WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.063 --> 00:00:03.250
- Oh, don't tell me you
broke another light bulb.

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:06.500
- Nope, I'm just putting
in an energy efficient LED.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.120
- But wait, aren't those more expensive?

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.850
- Yes, but going green can save you money.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.530
- Wait, so, it costs
more and saves you money?

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:14.915
- Yup.

00:00:14.915 --> 00:00:17.498
(upbeat music)

00:00:23.132 --> 00:00:24.110
(whooshing sound)

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.210
Going green, we've all heard about it,

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.610
but what does it actually mean?

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:30.820
Simply put, it's
protecting the environment

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.920
from harm and conserving
natural resources.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.140
These days, there are
lots of ways to do it.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.060
You can bike instead of drive.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:41.470
Eat organic, switch to
solar energy and so on.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:42.950
But this isn't new.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.740
In fact, saving energy has been around

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.405
for thousands of years.

00:00:47.405 --> 00:00:50.230
- Egypt used to take
old letters and use them

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:52.740
for money wrappings to save on papyri.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.410
British activists during
the Industrial Revolution,

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.750
successfully protested the construction

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.350
of railways that would
have ruined the land.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.540
There are many examples throughout history

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.600
of people protecting the environment,

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.400
whether they realized it or not.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.240
- And it wasn't until 1962

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.730
that the environmental
movement really took off,

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:14.330
with the release of Rachel
Carson's "Silent Spring".

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:16.960
This book informed the
world about pesticides

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.550
that could cause cancer
and threaten wildlife.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:21.030
This led to the formation

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.890
of the Environmental Protection Agency

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:25.890
and help inspire the very first Earth Day,

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.740
where 20 million people across America,

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.110
protested environmental destruction.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:34.570
- Since the 1970s, awareness
of environmental issues

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.610
has grown, along with
information and resources

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.390
helping people go green.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:42.310
Thankfully, some ways you
can reduce energy costs

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:45.030
nothing at all, like
recycling or turning off

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.980
the lights when you're not using them.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.790
But you may have also
noticed that many products

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:53.140
and solutions that help us
go green cost more money

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:56.170
than there are less environmentally
friendly competitors.

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:57.250
What's up with that?

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.480
- Well, sometimes the
product itself costs more

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.020
to make, that's the
case with organic food,

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:06.250
which reduces pollution but
also requires more labor

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:07.350
to produce.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.650
But that's not always the
case with green products.

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.430
Some charge more because, well, they can.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.610
Eco-friendly is
unfortunately sometimes used

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:19.450
as a marketing strategy to
appeal to wealthier shoppers.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.910
- But even with this
increased cost upfront,

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:25.040
going green can still save
you money in the long run.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.240
- How much exactly?

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:27.910
I think it's time to...

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:29.910
- [Both] Run the numbers!

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.827
(orchestral music)

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.660
- This is Sam.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.630
She's not the most
environmentally conscious person.

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:40.451
In fact, the thought of saving energy

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:42.720
never even crosses her mind.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.150
But one day Sam comes across a Ted talk

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.880
that gets her really fired
up to save the planet.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.050
How much money will she save?

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:51.600
Let's look at some examples.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.220
And heads up, there's a
lot of numbers this time.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:57.000
So, math nerds, you're welcome.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.150
- Okay, so currently Sam uses 75 watt

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:03.110
incandescent light bulbs
for five hours a day.

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:06.630
She has 40 of them in her
home and they cost $1 each.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:10.730
If she switches all 40 of
these bulbs to 11 watt LEDs

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:15.730
which costs $5 each, she has
to pay $160 more upfront.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.360
But because LEDs use less energy,

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.600
she'll save about $600 a year.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.040
That comes out to around $50 a month.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:27.240
So, in just over three
months, she'll make back

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.920
the initial $160 she
lost, pretty sweet deal.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:32.940
- Now let's see how much Sam will save

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.780
by switching shower heads.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.000
Currently, she uses one that pumps out

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.580
two and a half gallons
of water per minute.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:42.010
If she buys a low flow shower head,

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.580
it only use a gallon
and a half per minute.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:48.110
Let's say she takes seven
minute showers for 25 days

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:51.490
out of the month and uses
an electric water heater.

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:53.920
With her previous shower
head that comes out

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.150
to 438 gallons of water compared to 263

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:00.990
with her new one, pretty
impressive water savings.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:04.000
So, it must save a lot
of money too, right?

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.400
- Not exactly.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.520
This upgrade would only
save her $2 a month.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:11.710
- Which isn't a lot but
she could put those savings

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:14.040
towards a good cause like, I don't know,

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.280
supporting Two Cents on Patriot.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:17.180
- Not now, babe.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:18.250
- Sorry.

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:21.420
- Okay, so, what if Sam
switches to an electric car?

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.180
I mean, General Motors just announced

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:25.868
they're switching all their
cars to electric by 2035,

00:04:25.868 --> 00:04:27.470
so, why not Sam?

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:30.290
After all, she doesn't drive
the best car for the planet,

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.300
a 2010 Honda Accord.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.620
So, if she drives the
average number of miles,

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:36.499
paying $2.50 a gallon,

00:04:36.499 --> 00:04:41.130
she'll be looking at over
$1,300 a year in gas alone.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:44.740
But if she buys a 2021
Nissan Leaf electric car,

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:47.920
she'll pay just over
$500 a year to charge it,

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.630
that's over $800 a year in savings.

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:53.250
- And while some electric
cars cost a fortune

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:54.804
and can be more in insurance,

00:04:54.804 --> 00:04:58.010
a new Nissan Leaf is only around $31,000

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:00.200
and offers insurance
rates that are comparable

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.040
to many gas powered cars.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.770
Sam could also get the
federal tax incentive

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.060
knocking down the price even further.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:09.760
Plus, electric vehicles
cost thousands less

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:11.950
in maintenance and repairs
over their lifetime.

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:13.520
So, she could potentially save

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:17.310
around $300 a year on these costs alone.

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:19.650
- If Sam's not ready to
ditch her Honda, though,

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:20.970
she could still save money

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.000
by biking to work instead of driving,

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.540
and she'll get in her daily exercise too.

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:27.930
So, let's assume Sam is closer to work

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:30.850
than the average American
and it's 10 miles each way.

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:33.340
That's 100 a week or 400 miles a month,

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:35.350
she's not driving in her car.

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:37.870
After factoring in operating
costs and maintenance,

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:41.030
the bike costs about $6
a month to ride to work,

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:44.403
compared to around $53 a month
if she would have driven.

00:05:44.403 --> 00:05:48.980
That means Sam saves about
$47 a month by biking to work

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:51.680
which is over $550 a year.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:54.170
So, if her bike costs her $500,

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:57.050
she'll make that up and
savings in just under a year.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:59.170
And she might even get
a lower insurance rate

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:00.430
by driving less.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:03.230
Some insurance companies
offer 20% off or more

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:05.380
once you get below a
certain number of miles.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:07.510
Others offer smaller
discount or not at all,

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:09.640
it depends on company
demographics and in some cases

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:12.570
if you're willing to put
a tracker in your car.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:14.880
- And if Sam really wants to go all out,

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.240
she could get a solar panel.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.780
These generate their
own power from the sun

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:23.780
reducing electricity
costs by as much as 100%.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:25.900
Admittedly, it's a bit pricey.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:27.540
For a five kilowatt system,

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:31.450
it could cost her $20,000,
but she should be able

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:34.240
to get a 22% federal tax incentive,

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.550
knocking off nearly $4,500.

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:39.540
On top of that, she might
qualify for local rebates

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:40.540
and incentives.

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:43.840
So, now let's say we're
looking at $12,000.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:47.480
If the solar panels saves
her $100 a month on energy,

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:50.000
this will pay itself off in 10 years.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.300
Then she'll have little to no energy costs

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:55.370
for the remaining 15 years
of the system's life,

00:06:55.370 --> 00:06:56.520
how cool is that?

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:58.910
- So as you can see, sometimes going green

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:02.670
will save you a lot ,of money,
other times, not so much.

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.890
And in some cases you'll
save nothing at all.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.290
Like if you recycle or eat organic food.

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:11.630
So, why do it when it's not
financially beneficial to you?

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:14.560
- Because the truth is,
the environmental situation

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.580
is in critical condition.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:19.910
Climate change is causing
extreme weather events

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:22.350
like the snowpocalypse
we endured here in Texas

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.800
a couple months ago,
which could end up having

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.350
a $200 billion economic impact.

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:30.560
Hundreds of thousands of people die

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:32.870
from air pollution every year.

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:36.720
And over 500 species of land
animals are on the brink

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.420
of extinction because of the
human destruction of nature.

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:42.430
- These issues affect all of us.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:44.700
And while it's great that in so many cases

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:46.960
you'll end up paying less by going green,

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.660
that's not the only
reason to make the change.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:52.040
- Doing our part to fight climate change

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:55.120
and protect the planet
is worth doing regardless

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:57.240
of the personal savings.

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:58.850
- [Both] And that's our two cents.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:02.360
